\extable{Tp879_1}

Using the trapezoidal rule with four subintervals, find the approximate distance travelled by the particle in the interval $0\le t\le 1$.

solutions.latex:
The particle changes direction at $t=2$ (part (b)), so split the distance there. Each integral is a triangle under the velocity graph:

\exfigure{1369_q18_s1}
$$\begin{aligned}\text{distance travelled}&=\left|\int_0^2(8t-16)\,dt\right|+\int_2^5(8t-16)\,dt\\ &=\dfrac{1}{2}\times 2\times 16+\dfrac{1}{2}\times 3\times 24\\ &=16+36\\ &=52\text{ m}\end{aligned}$$

parts.latex:
The displacement of a car from a reference point along a straight road that runs east-west is $x$ kilometres. The car is initially 100 metres east of the reference point. The graph below shows the displacement of the car at time $t$ seconds.

\exfigure{1358_q9_1}

When the car is at point $P$, which statement reflects its motion?

solutions.latex:
\exfigure{1367_q28_s1}

The particle moves left until it stops at $t=\dfrac{\pi}{6}$, then moves right, so split the distance there:
$$\begin{aligned}d&=\left|\int_0^{\frac{\pi}{6}}(1-2\cos 2t)\,dt\right|+\int_{\frac{\pi}{6}}^{\frac{\pi}{2}}(1-2\cos 2t)\,dt\\ &=\left|\Big[t-\sin 2t\Big]_0^{\frac{\pi}{6}}\right|+\Big[t-\sin 2t\Big]_{\frac{\pi}{6}}^{\frac{\pi}{2}}\\ &=\left|\dfrac{\pi}{6}-\sin\dfrac{\pi}{3}-0\right|+\dfrac{\pi}{2}-\sin\pi-\left(\dfrac{\pi}{6}-\sin\dfrac{\pi}{3}\right)\\ &=\left|\dfrac{\pi}{6}-\dfrac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\right|+\dfrac{\pi}{2}-\left(\dfrac{\pi}{6}-\dfrac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\right)\\ &=\dfrac{\sqrt{3}}{2}-\dfrac{\pi}{6}+\dfrac{\pi}{2}-\dfrac{\pi}{6}+\dfrac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\\ &=\sqrt{3}+\dfrac{\pi}{6}\text{ metres}\end{aligned}$$

\exfigure{335_q24_s1}

The distance travelled is the area under the velocity–time graph, a trapezium with parallel sides 12 and 30 and height 5.
$$\begin{aligned} \text{Distance travelled}&=\text{area of trapezium}\\ &=\dfrac{5}{2}(12+30)\\ &=105\text{ metres} \end{aligned}$$

